course: biology
lesson: 311
title: Sharks by kind, small and strange: walking sharks, wobbegongs, cookiecutters and goblin sharks
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Not every shark is a big grey hunter like the one in Jaws. There are over 500 kinds, and many are small, flat, glowing or very strange.
explain: Some walk on their fins across rock pools, some lie on the seabed looking like a frilly carpet, and one tiny shark takes round bites out of whales as if with a cookie cutter.
explain: In the deep dark sea live sharks that glow, a pink shark that shoots its jaws out of its face, and a big gentle shark with a huge mouth that nobody knew about until 1976.
explain: Picture a beach after a storm: among the seaweed lies a black leathery pouch with curly corners. It is a mermaid's purse, an egg case that once held a baby shark or ray.
fact: Epaulette sharks of Australia and New Guinea walk between rock pools on their four paired fins, used like legs; there are about nine kinds of walking shark, all around New Guinea and northern Australia.
fact: Walking sharks can survive for hours with very little oxygen when a rock pool gets warm and stale at low tide.
fact: Wobbegongs are flat sharks with patterned skin and frilly flaps around the mouth; they lie still on the seabed and snap up fish that come too close.
fact: Wobbegongs are calm but will bite if stepped on or poked, and they can hold on tight; divers should look but never touch.
fact: The cookiecutter shark is only about 50 centimetres long; it latches on with sucking lips and spins to cut a round plug of flesh out of whales, dolphins, seals and big fish.
fact: The cookiecutter's belly glows to hide its shape against the light from above, except for a dark collar; some scientists think the collar lures big hunters close, but this is still argued.
fact: The goblin shark of the deep sea is pink because its blood shows through its thin skin, and it can fling its jaws forward out of its face to grab prey.
fact: The goblin shark's family goes back over 100 million years, so it is sometimes called a living fossil, and few people have seen one alive.
fact: The megamouth shark was first found in 1976, when one got tangled in a ship's sea anchor off Hawaii; it filters tiny shrimp with its huge mouth, and only a few hundred have ever been recorded.
fact: The dwarf lanternshark of the Caribbean deep is one of the smallest sharks, about 20 centimetres long, shorter than a 30-centimetre school ruler; it glows.
fact: Swell sharks gulp water to puff themselves up and wedge into rock cracks so a predator cannot pull them out.
fact: A mermaid's purse is the egg case of a catshark, skate or ray; curly tendrils at its four corners hold it to seaweed while the baby grows for months.
exercise: How does an epaulette shark get from one rock pool to the next? | It walks on its fins like legs | It jumps high out of the water like a dolphin | It rolls along the rocks like a ball | It waits for a big wave every time
exercise: How many fins does a walking shark use as legs? | Four, the two front and two back paired fins | Two, only the tail fins, which it swings from side to side | Six, one for each leg of an insect | One, the big fin on its back
exercise: How does a walking shark cope when a rock pool goes warm and stale? | It can survive hours with very little oxygen | It climbs a tree beside the rock pool to find fresh air | It breathes through a snorkel on its nose | It digs down to the cold sea floor
exercise: Where do walking sharks live? | Only around New Guinea and northern Australia | In every sea of the world, even under the Arctic ice | In rivers in Africa | In the North Sea near Britain
exercise: Why does a wobbegong have frilly flaps around its mouth? | They break up its outline so fish do not see it | They help it walk on land | They are extra gills for breathing air when out of the water | They are feelers for tasting sand
exercise: How does a wobbegong catch its food? | It lies still and snaps at fish that come close | It chases fish at high speed for miles across the open sea | It filters krill through its gills | It shoots water at insects above the sea
exercise: When are wobbegongs likely to bite a diver? | When someone steps on them or pokes them | Whenever they see a diver at all, even far away | Only at full moon | Never, they have no teeth
exercise: How long is a cookiecutter shark? | About 50 centimetres | About 5 metres, like a car | About 5 centimetres, like a little finger | About 15 metres, like a bus
exercise: How does the tiny cookiecutter bite a big whale? | It latches on with its lips and spins to cut out a round plug | It swallows the whale whole | It stings the whale with poison to make it fall asleep first | It waits until the whale dies
exercise: Why does the cookiecutter's belly glow? | To hide its shape against the light from above | To read in the dark deep sea | To keep itself warm | To scare away hungry whales with a sudden bright flash
exercise: Why does a goblin shark look pink? | Its blood shows through its thin skin | It eats only pink shrimp and turns their colour | It is sunburnt from swimming near the surface | It is covered in pink sand from the sea floor
exercise: What can a goblin shark do with its jaws? | Fling them forward out of its face to grab prey | Take them off and leave them on rocks | Use them like legs to walk along the seabed | Fold them into its gills
exercise: Why is the goblin shark sometimes called a living fossil? | Its family goes back over 100 million years | It is made of stone | It was dug up alive from rock by a team of miners | It lives only in museums
exercise: How was the megamouth shark first found in 1976? | One got tangled in a ship's sea anchor off Hawaii | A child saw it swimming past a pier in London | It was found frozen in Arctic ice | It was dug up as a fossil in a desert
exercise: What does a megamouth eat? | Tiny shrimp, filtered with its huge mouth | Seals and sea lions | Seaweed growing on rocks | Other sharks, even ones much bigger than itself
exercise: About how long is a dwarf lanternshark? | About 20 centimetres | About 2 metres, like a door | About 2 centimetres, like a bee | About 12 metres, like a bus
exercise: How does a swell shark stay safe from a predator? | It gulps water, swells up and wedges itself into a rock crack | It swims faster than any other fish | It sprays a cloud of dark ink, like an octopus, and hides | It plays dead on the beach
exercise: What is a mermaid's purse? | The egg case of a catshark, skate or ray | A bag a mermaid lost at sea | A dried-up jellyfish | The old skin a shark sheds and leaves behind every year
test: «saw a vid of a shark WALKING lol fake right» What is the best reply? | Probably real; epaulette sharks walk between rock pools on their fins | Fake, sharks die the moment they touch rocks | Fake, no fish has ever moved over rock | Real, but only sharks in zoos can do it, after they are trained by keepers
test: Count them: 3 epaulette sharks walk across a reef using their paired fins as legs. How many fin-legs are moving? | 12 | 6 | 3 | 9
test: Count them: you find 2 mermaid's purses on the beach. How many curly corners do they have together? | 8 | 4 | 2 | 6
test: A mate wades out and nearly steps on a lumpy patterned carpet on the seabed. What should he do? | Back off and not touch it; it may be a wobbegong that bites if trodden on | Pick it up to take home | Stamp on it to see if it moves | Pull its frilly beard to check that it is only an old rug
test: «look this dolphin's got perfect round holes in it, like someone used a cookie cutter» What most likely made them? | A cookiecutter shark's spinning bite | A boat propeller drawing perfect circles | A great white shark taking a single bite | Bad sunburn on the dolphin's thin skin
test: What would happen if a goblin shark could not shoot its jaws forward? | It would miss more fast prey in the dark | It would have to start eating seaweed off the rocks | It would stop being pink | It would have to live in rivers
test: In a cartoon every shark is a huge grey monster with a fin sticking out of the water. What is the truth? | Many sharks are small, flat, glowing or strange; some are shorter than your arm | All sharks are at least 5 metres long | Only great white sharks are real sharks, and the others are just big fish | Sharks are always grey, never patterned
test: Why could a shark as big as the megamouth stay hidden until 1976? | It lives in the vast deep sea where few people look | It was invisible until then | It only appeared in 1976, when it suddenly evolved from another shark | It hid in rivers away from the sea
test: Count it: a whale shark can be about 60 times as long as a dwarf lanternshark. If a lanternshark is 20 centimetres, about how long is the whale shark? | About 12 metres | About 1.2 metres | About 60 metres long | About 80 centimetres
test: «why would a fish get FATTER to stay safe, makes no sense» What is the answer for a swell shark? | Swelling up wedges it tight in a crack so it cannot be pulled out | Fat fish taste bad to every predator | It floats up into the air like a balloon and drifts away from danger | It turns into a rock when it swells
test: Why might a little shark that glows on its belly be harder to spot from below? | Its glow matches the light coming down from the surface | Glowing makes animals see-through | The glow blinds every hunter for ever, so none can see it again | Light from below is always dark
exam: Identify the principal means by which the epaulette shark proceeds between rock pools at low tide. | By ambulation upon its paired pectoral and pelvic fins | By leaping from the water by means of its tail | By rolling its body along the substrate | By remaining stationary until the tide returns
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the cookiecutter shark. | A shark of approximately half a metre removes circular portions of flesh from substantially larger animals | A shark of approximately ten metres consumes whales in their entirety | A shark of approximately half a metre subsists exclusively on seaweed | A shark of approximately five metres attacks only vessels
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the discovery of the megamouth shark? | It was first recorded in 1976 upon becoming entangled in a vessel's sea anchor near Hawaii | It was first recorded in 1776 by fishermen in the North Sea | It has been known since antiquity as a common coastal species | It was first identified solely from fossilised teeth discovered in the deserts of North Africa
exam: Ascertain the nature of the object commonly designated a «mermaid's purse». | The egg case of a catshark, skate or ray, anchored to seaweed by tendrils | The discarded skin of an adult shark | A form of seaweed resembling a pouch, in which small fish shelter from storms | The shell of a deep-sea crab
